every weekend here on c-span - this book  a g-man's journal is about oliver  Ravel's 30 year career in the FBI he  directed or participated in major  investigations such as the JFK  assassination the Watergate break-in and  the Iran hostage crisis last month he  spoke about the book and his experiences  while at a Barnes &amp; Noble bookstore in  Washington DC he's introduced by  bookstore spokeswoman Jane Graff  tonight we're very pleased to welcome  all of you my name is Jane Graf welcome  to Barnes &amp; Noble Georgetown and welcome  to all of our c-span viewers across the  country from 1964 to 1994 buck Ravel  directed or participated directly in  virtually every major FBI investigation  from the JFK assassination Watergate the  Iran hostage crisis and the Gulf War  anti-terrorist operations is the former  associate deputy director in charge of  investigations the bureau's number two  career post he sealed his reputation as  a legendary maverick in federal law  enforcement now in a landmark  autobiography Revell candidly reveals  the brilliance and deep rooted flaws  behind 30 years of an FBI investigations  known early on for his street-smart  proactive and hard-hitting style inside  J edgar Hoover's Bureau Revell hunted  down mafia hitmen Ku Klux Klan type  terrorist group in Eastern black spies  quickly rising in a system that was  often its own worst enemy he discloses  why after Hoover's death in 1972 he and  other reform-minded agents moved to  modernize the FBI both tactically and  technologically and how he developed  investigative strategies innovated  specific surveillance methods and  innovated and championed special FBI  units such as special events management  and hostage rescue as skilled as advice  let's try that again  it's skilled as advising heads of state  as colouring criminals Revell was  summoned to the White House Situation  Room is the senior FBI representative to  the National Security Council he emerged  as one of our nation's leading  authorities on terrorism and national  security issues he recounts how he  proceeded persuaded Congress to enact  federal laws regarding fugitive  apprehension how he directed all  international operations including  tracking the kidnappers and killers of  hostages in Beirut International mafia  weapons smuggling and French and  Japanese industrial espionage he also  demonstrates why it is vital for the FBI  to become a global nerve center for the  world's law enforcement agencies on the  home front buck Ravel continues to  advise the National Security Council the  Pentagon  and Justice Department's on matters of  organized crime drug enforcement  white-collar crime and terrorism in his  new memoirs a g-man's journal he  proposes drastic reforms for improving  the current system and law enforcement  and justice and bluntly analyzes what  went wrong at incidents like Branch  Davidian tragedy and Wako World Trade  Center bombing in the Oklahoma City  bombing a riveting chronicle of an  extraordinary career amid history  shaping events a g-man's journal lays  bare the varied criminal threads facing  America today and sets the  crime-fighting agenda for the future  ladies and gentlemen please give me a  warm barnes ennoble welcome for oliver  buck rebel it's hard hearing all those  things that the publisher has written  and then try and come up and justify  them particularly with my colleagues  that I've worked with sitting here over  the years this book was of course a  joint effort not only my wife and many  of my former colleagues but with a young  author that helped me take government  ease and change it into a form of  written language that the public would  better understand I had never written a  first-person report or novel or anything  of this nature so writing in the first  person in an interesting fashion was one  of my first challenges and the Dwight  Williams my co-author was very very  helpful in that regard and of course my  wife Sharon made sure that I made  everything correct and accurate and  helped my memory which over the years is  not as keen as it as it once was I  actually wrote this book for several  reasons one it was not my idea I was  approached by a book producer and  convinced to do the project secondly it  was to try and bring balance to the  historical record of many of the event  events that I was fortunate enough to be  able to participate in from as the title  says the Kennedy assassination all the  way to the Oklahoma City bombing such  things as Watergate the iran-contra  affair the Pan Am 103 investigation  worlds  then largest terrorist investigation the  entire effort against the Klan and the  elements that were espousing hate  throughout the south and other parts of  our nation during the the Civil Rights  Act even in those elements of that  movement who moved into criminal  violations and attempted to essentially  bring our government to a halt and and  in fact engaged in many instances and  murder and bombings to carry out their  philosophical goals those things happen  at a time in our society when our  country was not sure where it was going  or how it wanted to get there and one  agency in reality was in the midst of  all of this turmoil and it was the FBI  starting with its legendary and longtime  director J Edgar Hoover who of course  died in 1972 and then trying to come  through the transition with Watergate  occurring right after he died  on into the the current events that  you're all familiar with that is  occurring on our on our national scene  just today an individual was indicted  for the Atlanta Olympic Park bombing a  case that is of course been going on now  for two and a half years the Unabomber  was finally identified and apprehended  at an espionage agent a former NSA  analyst was indicted yesterday so the  events and activities that the FBI is  deeply involved in are very important to  our society and it was my intention to  try and bring some balance to what has  become the the norm which is to bash  federal agents and agencies to make them  responsible for many of the ills of our  society and to blame them for a great  deal of the difficulty that we face  whether or not there's any substance to  that at all now in this book I do not  excuse the mistakes that have been made  and there have been many an agency with  25,000 people will make mistakes it has  and will make some in the future but I  try and point out the exceptional  dedication and work that the special  agents and other employees of the FBI  bring to their country  the contributions they make and indeed  the very significant level of safety and  security that they bring to our society  I've had an opportunity to travel all  around the world and I can tell you no  other country has an organization like  the FBI nowhere else can a organization  investigate at the head of state no  where else can they investigate sitting  judges are members of the legislature as  has the FBI and done so without any  political agenda in spite of the Oliver  Stone scenarios that you see coming out  of Hollywood today today's FBI the  twenty seven or eight thousand people in  it are just as dedicated as those that I  joined in nineteen sixty four when I  entered the agency so in large part  although this story is based around  around my career it is really the story  of the FBI as it went through a  transition from the years of j edgar  Hoover who built the organization and  who in his latter years brought it into  controversy to the current FBI which is  expanding its responsibilities on a  global basis and is a very important  force to see that terrorism  international organized crime which is  spreading and proliferating drug  trafficking and other internationally  linked criminal organizations are  thwarted in their efforts to essentially  take what was the dichotomy of the Cold  War and what President Bush called the  new order and turned it into a world  disorder and world Anarchy because in  the 21st century the first line of  defense in my mind is going to be  federal law enforcement to protect us  from the international drug traffickers  from the interlinked organized crime  groups from the terrorist organizations  which now have grown up not only with  state sponsors but also now with very  strong philosophical basis to where they  want to maximize the casualties and  punish America for being the opposition  the the so-called Great Satan these are  the events that will challenge us in the  future and with weapons of mass  destruction looming not on the horizon  but actually available now  the two groups organizations and  individuals it is imperative that we  have a line of defense that is effective  and yet recognizes that what makes us  different is the rule of law and the  constitutional application of that law  is the first order of business for the  FBI I was pleased to be able to serve  the FBI and my country for 30 years  before that for five years as a Marine  officer so I wanted to be able to impart  some of that experience the involvement  of my family and how much they have  given and committed over the years and  the successes that we've had and some of  the failures that we've suffered and the  reasons for and not an explanation but  an attempt to balance the scales so that  people will understand that human beings  do make mistakes but on the whole and  given the circumstances they face the  FBI is a very very remarkable  organization I think that I look back on  this on my career and people say well  what was your most important case what  was the the case that brought you the  greatest satisfaction and I can't really  identify that case because so many did  bring that level of satisfaction whether  it was returning a child to its parents  which I had an opportunity to do on some  occasions whether it was of apprehending  a mafia hitman who had killed dozens of  people whether it was tracking down  spies who jeopardized our national  security or whether it was bringing in  drug traffickers my last arrest in  Dallas Texas before I retired was of a  Mexican drug trafficker whose  organization had killed more than a  dozen people his bodyguard was  personally responsible for killing 18  people and he was preying upon the young  people in Dallas bringing drugs in from  Mexico and getting them hooked so that  they could continue to develop their  drug trafficking organization those kind  of accomplishments are not individual  they're of the organization and the  people that support it like the US  Attorney's but they still give you a  great deal of satisfaction because you  know that if you're not there  then a difference would not have been  made and they do the agents and  employees of the FBI do indeed make a  difference I think that on balance I'll  be very satisfied if this book at least  starts a dialogue among some as to the  importance of federal law enforcement  the need to redirect and restructure and  reorganize federal law enforcement to  deal with the threats of the 21st  century and it recognizes the deep  professional commitment of the career  employees of the FBI and the other  federal agencies and gives them the  appropriate tools to carry out their  duties and functions that of course is  the hope and goal of this book so with  that I'd like to at this time respond to  questions and hopefully things I haven't  covered I'll be able to answer for you  thank you area of focus is right now as  far as law enforcement and should that  be changed  well the principle for the FBI cannot  have a principal focus because it has so  many responsibilities across the entire  spectrum of a federal law enforcement  but then can have areas of emphasis and  we saw that today with the indictment of  rudolph for the Atlanta Olympic bombing  certainly terrorism is a an area of  significant responsibility the  investigations ongoing in East Africa  with the Osama bin Laden organization is  an area of principle FBI responsibility  so both domestic and foreign or  international terrorism have to be areas  of very specific interest and effort on  the part of the FBI  of course the FBI is the principal  counterintelligence agency of the United  States  so this because the Cold War is over  does not mean that we do not have  enemies both state and individual who  would take advantage of our technology  take advantage of our national security  information and turn it against our best  interest so espionage and  counterintelligence will continue to be  a very important responsibility I  believe the FBI and the DEA must do a  better job in strategizing on a global  basis the entire drug effort drug the  effort the FBI knew when we entered Drug  Enforcement in 1982 we could not solve  the drug problem what we knew was that  we had to be a holding action until our  society could come to grips with this  terrible plague that's been laid upon us  education and treatment have to be part  of the process but we also must have  federal agencies at work or in a  coordinated fashion to ensure that we  can prevent the drugs from entering the  United States and deal with the major  organizations who are trafficking drugs  to our children and essentially  destroying our next generation that must  be an effort and of course the bottom  line responsibility has to always be to  deal with corruption particularly  corruption in government the FBI has a  very unique and important responsibility  to deal with corruption at every level  of government whether it be a local  judge a county commissioner are a  federal a United States Senator are a  federal judge all of the  things have happened in the past and I'm  certain they'll happen in the future but  you must have an agency totally free  from partisan involvement or political  activity in order to do that I think  those are the continuing challenges at  the FBI faces yes sir  Klansmen Tommy Terence later turned  against the plan has 